  "notes": [
    "Differences are this project minus the map, in percentage points of time. At the two IM-2 points this project gives 44.3 to 44.4 % of time in sunlight over 18.6 years at 0 m and 44.8 to 45.0 % at 2 m, against 46.5 to 46.8 % in the PGDA map (-2.4 to -2.1 points at 0 m, -1.8 to -1.7 at 2 m).",
    "At the three polar check points, which are sharp summits, the answer depends strongly on the height above the ground: at 0 m this project gives 43.9 to 54.0 % against 82.7 to 88.4 % in the PGDA map (-43.2 to -28.7 points), at 2 m it gives 81.0 to 84.9 % (-5.2 to -1.7 points).",
    "The 0 m gap is likely a matter of scale rather than of the Sun's position: on the 10 m terrain the horizon of an observer at 0 m is the ground within 20 m in 48.6 to 65.1 % of directions, detail that a 60 m map pixel cannot hold. As a test, averaging the terrain around each point to the 60 m pixel of the map raises the 0 m result to 77.3 to 78.2 %; a different version of the elevation model (the map dates from 2016, our terrain from 2023) may explain part of the rest, which was not tested.",
    "The LROC map, made from photographs of the surface in 2010-2011, shows 47.4 to 48.2 % at the same three points: this project's 0 m values differ from it by -4.4 to +6.4 points and its 2 m values by +33.2 to +37.2 points. This is consistent with a camera recording whether the ground itself is lit, but the LROC pixel is 100 m and its brightness threshold is conservative, so the agreement is indicative only.",
    "The definition of 'lit' matters little for the Sun: the visible fraction of the disk and the disk centre differ by at most 0.4 points and one year (2010-2011) differs from 18.6 years by at most 0.4 points, while counting any visible part of the disk adds 1.5 to 7.1 points. For the Earth this project (centre of the Earth above the horizon, 0 m) is -14.7 to -4.6 points from the PGDA Earth map; counting a body as visible when any part of its disk shows, for an observer at 2 m, comes within 0.2 points of the Earth map and 1.2 points of the Sun map at all five points. This suggests that the maps count partial visibility, which we could not confirm beyond the product page.",
    "An independent recomputation of the horizon from the same elevation data reproduces the stored masks to within 0.000 degrees at 2 m and 0.000 degrees at 0 m. The first run of this check found differences of up to 0.36 degrees at 0 m, caused by single-precision rounding of terrain heights inside the mask calculation; that was fixed and the masks were rebuilt."
  ],
